        history: {
          origins: "Part of the North of England's deep-rooted pub game culture. While Yorkshire developed its own league standards, Lancashire refined the 'Low' rule to make the game more aggressive and trick-focused.",
          originCountries: [
            'England',
          ],
          timeline: [
            '1800s: All Fours becomes a staple in Northern English pubs.',
            "1970s: Documented by Arthur Taylor in 'Pub Games' as a living tradition in Blackburn.",
            'Present: Still played in localized pub leagues and social clubs.',
          ],
          evolution: "The removal of 'The Beg' and the shift of the 'Low' point to a 'captured' status are the defining evolutionary steps that distinguish it from the 17th-century progenitor.",
          cultural: "In Blackburn, the first lead is often decided by a 'Red or Black' guess after the cut. It's a game of heavy social interaction and 'pegging' on boards.",
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        setup: {
          players: '4 players in two fixed partnerships (North-South vs East-West).',
          deck: 'Standard 52-card deck. Ace is high.',
          equipment: 'Standard deck, All Fours pegging board (traditionally to 11 points).',
          dealing: 'Each player receives 6 cards in batches of 2 or 3. No card is turned up for trumps.',
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        rules: {
          objective: 'First partnership to 11 points by scoring High, Low (taken), Jack, and Game.',
          gameplay: "1. **The Pitch**: The player to the dealer's left (the Pitcher) leads any card. The suit of this card immediately becomes trumps for the hand. 2. **Trick Play**: Follow suit if possible. You may trump instead of following suit, even if you hold a card of the led suit. 3. **The 'Low' Hunt**: Unlike other variants, the 'Low' point is awarded to the team that WINS the trick containing the lowest trump card that was dealt in that hand. 4. **Scoring Order**: Points are counted and pegged in the following order: High, Low, Jack, Game.",
          keyRules: [
            'High (1 pt): Awarded to the team of the player dealt the highest trump.',
            'Low (1 pt): Awarded to the team that WINS the trick containing the lowest trump card dealt.',
            'Jack (1 pt): Awarded to the team that WINS the trick containing the Jack of trumps.',
            "Game (1 pt): Awarded to the team with the most 'pips' in their captured tricks (Ace=4, King=3, Queen=2, Jack=1, 10=10).",
            "Pitcher's partner must not look at their cards until the first card (The Pitch) is led.",
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: "If you hold a Ten, try to throw it on your partner's winning trick. If you hold the Jack of trumps, hide it until you are sure your partner can protect it.",
          intermediate: "The Low Strategy: Since 'Low' is taken, not dealt, you must win the trick it's in. If you have the 2 of trumps, don't lead it; hold it as an escape card or wait until you can win a trick with a higher trump to secure it.",
          advanced: "Pitch Manipulation: If you are the Pitcher and hold the Ace and Ten of a suit, lead the Ace. You've fixed the trumps and secured 4 card-points toward 'Game' in one move.",
          tips: [
            'Always count the trumps played. With only 24 cards dealt, knowing the remaining trumps is easier than in other games.',
            "In Lancashire, winning the Low point (the 'Taken Low') is often the difference between winning and losing a match.",
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        variations: {
          list: [
            {
              id: 'lancashire-league',
